    <article>
      <section id="problem" class="lt">
        <p class="eyebrow">The problem</p>
        <h2>Seven in ten orders stop at one product.</h2>
        <p>Basil already sells a bottle, a school bag and a dip in the same print as the lunchbox. The page never makes the case for buying them together, and the discount that should do it sits in a box of rules.</p>
        <div class="role"><span class="k">My role</span><p>Solo and self directed. I audited the live page, picked the lever, designed the section for desktop and mobile, wrote every line of copy, and planned how to measure it.</p></div>
        <div class="stats">
          <div><b>70%</b><span>of yourbasil.com orders carry one product, so only 30% carry two or more.</span></div>
          <div><b>₹640</b><span>what a full Pixie Dash set saves under the Buy 3 get 10% offer that already runs site wide.</span></div>
          <div><b>₹0</b><span>new discount cost. The proposal only makes the existing offer visible at the right moment.</span></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="who" class="lt wide">
        <h2>Who has the problem</h2>
        <div class="goals three">
          <div><span class="k">Primary user</span><h3>The parent buying on a phone</h3><p>Shopping for a child in grades 1 to 5, usually around a new school term. By the time they reach Add to cart they have already chosen the lunchbox and the print. Nothing tells them the bottle in the same print would unlock 5% on the whole order.</p></div>
          <div><span class="k">The one who decides</span><h3>The child who picks the print</h3><p>Kids do not pick a lunchbox. They pick Pixie Dash. The character is the real purchase, and it already runs across the box, the bottle and the bag.</p></div>
          <div><span class="k">The business</span><h3>Basil</h3><p>Pays for Buy 2 get 5% and Buy 3 get 10% on every qualifying order. Today that spend does no persuading, because a parent only sees it as a rule in a box below the button.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="why" class="lt wide">
        <h2>Why it matters</h2>
        <div class="goals">
          <div><h3>Order value is capped by the page, not by demand</h3><p>A parent buying a ₹2,099 lunchbox for school needs a bottle and a bag anyway. If those come from somewhere else, the matching set Basil designed never gets sold as a set.</p></div>
          <div><h3>A paid incentive that nobody sees</h3><p>Every discount rupee Basil gives away should be moving a basket. At the moment it mostly lands on orders that would have been two items anyway.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="audit" class="lt wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">The live page today</p>
          <h2>Basil already has a kit block and a Buy 2, Buy 3 offer. They just never talk to each other.</h2>
          <p>Audited live on the Bento ACE 3C, Pixie Dash page, 24 September 2026, reading the buy box in the order a phone renders it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="audit">
          <figure class="bbox">
            <span class="k">Current buy box, top to bottom</span>
            <ol>
              <li>Gallery</li>
              <li>Title, rating, ₹2,099 (MRP ₹3,500)</li>
              <li>Choose your Vibe · 10 prints</li>
              <li>Personalise for ₹149</li>
              <li class="btn-row">Quantity + Add to cart</li>
              <li>Trust row · FDA, free shipping, warranty, returns</li>
              <li class="amb">Available offers · Buy 3 get 10%, Buy 2 get 5%, EMI, countdown</li>
              <li class="amb">Complete the Kit · HydraPop ₹1,299, Pickle Dip ₹149, each with its own Add to cart</li>
            </ol>
            <figcaption>Amber blocks are the two AOV tools. Both sit below the button that ends the decision.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <div class="finds">
            <div><span class="n">01</span><h3>The offer and the kit never meet</h3><p>Buy 2 get 5% and Buy 3 get 10% live in one box as rules. Complete the Kit lives in another box under it. A parent who adds the bottle is never told they just unlocked 5%, so the discount does no persuading.</p></div>
            <div><span class="n">02</span><h3>The kit shows up after the decision</h3><p>On a phone it sits below Add to cart, the trust row and the offers box. By the time a parent scrolls to it, they have usually already tapped the button and gone to the cart.</p></div>
            <div><span class="n">03</span><h3>Three items means three separate decisions</h3><p>Each kit card has its own Add to cart. There is no running total and no single action for the set, so every extra item has to win its own argument.</p></div>
            <div><span class="n">04</span><h3>The ₹149 dip cannot unlock the offer</h3><p>Offers apply to products above ₹149. The Pickle Dip is exactly ₹149, so the cheapest thing in the kit does not count toward Buy 2, and nothing on the page says so.</p></div>
            <div><span class="n">05</span><h3>The strongest match is missing</h3><p>The Wander school bag comes in Pixie Dash at ₹2,999 and is not in the kit. It is the one item that turns a lunch purchase into a back to school purchase.</p></div>
            <div class="keep"><h3>What I would keep exactly as it is</h3><p>Free shipping on every order, so a free shipping threshold is not a lever here and I did not design one. The character worlds, because Pixie Dash, Super Swoosh and the rest already run across lunchboxes, bottles and bags. That is the asset this whole proposal is built on.</p></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="lever" class="wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">The lever, and why this one</p>
          <h2>Kids do not pick a lunchbox. They pick Pixie Dash.</h2>
          <p>Every Basil print is a character world that already runs across the bento, the bottle and the bag. So the obvious second product is not whatever a recommendation engine guesses. It is the same character in the next thing on the school checklist. The section sells that set, and prices it with the Buy 2 and Buy 3 offer Basil already runs, so it adds no new discount cost.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="set">
          <figure><img src="img/03-bento.webp" width="347" height="346" alt="Bento ACE 3C lunchbox in the Pixie Dash print" loading="lazy"><figcaption><b>Bento ACE 3C</b><span>₹2,099 · Pixie Dash</span></figcaption></figure>
          <i aria-hidden="true">+</i>
          <figure><img src="img/03-bottle.webp" width="346" height="346" alt="HydraPop 600ml bottle in the Pixie Dash print" loading="lazy"><figcaption><b>HydraPop 600ml</b><span>₹1,299 · Pixie Dash</span></figcaption></figure>
          <i aria-hidden="true">+</i>
          <figure><img src="img/03-bag.webp" width="347" height="346" alt="Wander school bag in the Pixie Dash print" loading="lazy"><figcaption><b>Wander school bag</b><span>₹2,999 · Pixie Dash</span></figcaption></figure>
          <div class="total"><span class="k">The full set</span><s>₹6,397</s><b>₹5,757</b><p>Buy 3 get 10% applied. You save ₹640.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="two">
          <div>
            <h3 class="sub">Levers I considered</h3>
            <div class="options">
              <div class="opt built"><h3>Matching set, tied to the Buy 2 and Buy 3 offer</h3><span class="tag">Chosen</span><p>Uses the brand’s strongest asset and a discount that already exists.</p></div>
              <div class="opt sec"><h3>A ₹149 add on that fits inside the box</h3><span class="tag">Secondary</span><p>Designed next to the compartment details, where the fit question gets asked.</p></div>
              <div class="opt"><h3>One for each kid, a second box in another print</h3><span class="tag">Parked</span><p>Strong for families with siblings. Worth testing once the set is live.</p></div>
              <div class="opt"><h3>Free shipping threshold</h3><span class="tag">Not needed</span><p>Shipping is already free on every order, so there is no gap to pull people across.</p></div>
              <div class="opt"><h3>A You may also like carousel</h3><span class="tag">Rejected</span><p>Gives no reason to buy together and pulls attention off the product the parent came for.</p></div>
              <div class="opt"><h3>Pre ticked add ons</h3><span class="tag">Rejected</span><p>Lifts AOV on day one, then costs trust and returns for a brand whose line is Approved by Families.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="sub">The behaviour it is built on</h3>
            <div class="beh">
              <div><h3>Endowed progress</h3><p>The lunchbox on the page already counts as 1 of 3, so the parent starts a third of the way to the best price instead of at zero.</p></div>
              <div><h3>Goal gradient</h3><p>People push harder the closer the next reward is. The bar never says a percentage to chase, it says exactly one more item.</p></div>
              <div><h3>Set completion</h3><p>A matching set feels unfinished at two out of three. The third slot is visibly empty, in the same print.</p></div>
              <div><h3>Savings in rupees</h3><p>At a ₹5,000 basket, You save ₹640 lands harder than 10% off. The number is shown live and it moves.</p></div>
              <div><h3>Fewer, better choices</h3><p>Two curated add ons in the chosen print, not a carousel of twelve. Nothing to compare, only yes or no.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="desktop" class="lt wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">The solution · Desktop</p>
          <h2>One set builder, right after the print is picked, above the button.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="dsk">
          <figure><a class="zoom-in" href="img/04-desktop.webp" aria-label="Open the desktop page at full size"><img class="shot wide" src="img/04-desktop.webp" width="1600" height="2267" alt="The Bento ACE 3C, Pixie Dash product page on desktop with the Make it a Pixie Dash set section above Add to cart, and the Pickle Dip card beside the compartment diagram, numbered 1 to 5" loading="lazy"></a></figure>
          <div>
            <span class="k">What changed on the page</span>
            <div class="finds">
              <div><span class="n">1</span><h3>The set builder moves above the button</h3><p>It sits right after the vibe picker, where the parent has just committed to a character. The old offers box and kit block are folded into it.</p></div>
              <div><span class="n">2</span><h3>The offer becomes a progress bar</h3><p>The lunchbox already fills the first segment. The line above the bar says exactly what the next piece is worth.</p></div>
              <div><span class="n">3</span><h3>Two curated pieces, same print</h3><p>The bottle and the bag in Pixie Dash, nothing else. Nothing is pre ticked. Each row says why it belongs with this box.</p></div>
              <div><span class="n">4</span><h3>One button for the whole set</h3><p>Add 3 to cart · ₹5,757 is one decision instead of three separate taps, with the live total in the label.</p></div>
              <div><span class="n">5</span><h3>A ₹149 add on where the fit question lives</h3><p>Below the fold, beside the compartment diagram. It says up front that it does not count toward the offer.</p></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="mobile" class="lt wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">The solution · Mobile, full length</p>
          <h2>On a phone the set lands before the tap, and a sticky bar keeps it in view.</h2>
        </div>
        <figure class="mobfull">
          <div class="iph noisl"><div class="sc" tabindex="0" aria-label="Scroll the full mobile page"><img src="img/05-mobile-full.webp" width="780" height="5553" alt="The full mobile product page, top to bottom: gallery, title and price, the vibe picker, Make it a Pixie Dash set with the bottle ticked and Add 2 to cart for ₹3,228, product features, the compartment diagram with the ₹149 Pickle Dip, reviews, and the sticky set bar" loading="lazy"></div></div>
          <ol class="ms-notes"><li><span class="n">01</span><h3>The print, then the set</h3><p>Choose your Vibe is followed straight away by Make it a Pixie Dash set. Today the kit sits below Add to cart, the trust row and the offers box.</p></li><li><span class="n">02</span><h3>5% unlocked, the gap in rupees</h3><p>With the bottle ticked, the line switches from the next reward to the gap between 5% and 10%: save ₹640 instead of ₹170.</p></li><li><span class="n">03</span><h3>The dip, and the sticky set bar</h3><p>The ₹149 dip sits by the compartments. Once the set scrolls away, a bar holds the count, the next saving and the button.</p></li></ol>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <section id="states" class="lt wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">States and edge cases</p>
          <h2>The section has to stay honest in every state, including the awkward ones.</h2>
          <p>Core states first: how the bar, the line above it, the saving and the button all move together. Then the cases that break a naive version of this: sold out prints, items already in the cart, prints with no matching pieces, and personalised boxes.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="states">
          <figure><span class="k">A · Landing, nothing added</span><img src="img/06-state-a.webp" width="577" height="986" alt="State A: set of 1 piece, Add to cart ₹2,099" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The box already fills one segment. Nothing is pre ticked.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure><span class="k">B · Bottle added, 5% unlocked</span><img src="img/06-state-b.webp" width="579" height="986" alt="State B: set of 2, save ₹170, Add 2 to cart ₹3,228" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The line switches from the next reward to the gap between 5% and 10%, in rupees.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure><span class="k">C · Full set, 10% applied</span><img src="img/06-state-c.webp" width="579" height="957" alt="State C: set of 3, save ₹640, Add 3 to cart ₹5,757" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Bar turns green, the saving becomes the headline, the button carries the count.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure><span class="k">B2 · Bag added first</span><img src="img/06-state-b2.webp" width="579" height="986" alt="State B2: bag ticked first, save ₹255, Add 2 to cart ₹4,843" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Order does not matter. The copy names whichever piece is still missing.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
        <h3 class="sub">Edge cases</h3>
        <div class="states">
          <figure><span class="k">D · Matching piece sold out</span><img src="img/06-state-d.webp" width="577" height="855" alt="State D: HydraPop sold out in Pixie Dash with Notify me, bag still available" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The row stays so the set still reads as a set, but it cannot be ticked. The copy stops promising 10% and points at the piece that is actually available. No swapping to another print without asking, because the print is the reason to buy.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure><span class="k">E · Piece already in the cart</span><img src="img/06-state-e.webp" width="579" height="855" alt="State E: bottle already in the cart, 5% unlocked, Add to cart ₹2,099" loading="lazy"><figcaption>The bar reads the cart, not just this section. Counting it twice would double the order, ignoring it would understate the saving. The button only adds what is new.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure><span class="k">F · A print with no matching pieces</span><img src="img/06-state-f.webp" width="579" height="626" alt="State F: Complete the lunch kit with the Pickle Dip for a Classic Black box" loading="lazy"><figcaption>When a print has no bottle or bag, the heading drops the character promise and falls back to pieces that suit any print. The section never shows a mismatched character to fill space. In production it should pull neutral pieces above ₹149, so they count toward the offer.</figcaption></figure>
          <figure><span class="k">G · Personalised box</span><img src="img/06-state-g.webp" width="579" height="965" alt="State G: box personalised with Aarya at ₹2,248, with a note that name printed boxes cannot be returned" loading="lazy"><figcaption>Basil already warns that customised orders are not returnable. The set makes that warning more important, because now it applies to one piece and not the others. The note says exactly which.</figcaption></figure>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="rationale" class="lt wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">Design rationale</p>
          <h2>Why it is built this way</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="goals">
          <div><h3>Why this section</h3><p>Parents do not dislike Basil’s other products. Nothing on the page gives them a reason to buy two together. The character is that reason, and Basil already knows it: there is a Bento Neo plus HydraPop bundle in Pixie Dash on the site. This brings the idea to every PDP, for whatever print the parent picks.</p><p>The incentive is already paid for. Buy 2 and Buy 3 stop being rules and become progress a parent can watch fill up.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Why this placement</h3><p>Directly after the vibe picker and above Add to cart. That is the moment the parent has just chosen a character, and the last moment before the decision ends. The current kit sits after the button, which on a phone means after the tap.</p><p>The ₹149 dip goes lower, beside the compartment diagram, because that is where its question gets asked: will the curd leak into the roti?</p></div>
          <div><h3>What I removed</h3><p>The separate Available offers box and the old Complete the Kit block, so there is one place to understand the deal.</p><p>The offer countdown. It is a timer saved in the browser that quietly restarts at zero, so the deadline is never real. For a brand whose line is Approved by Families, I dropped it rather than design around it.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Trade offs</h3><p><strong>Curated over complete.</strong> Two pieces in the chosen print, not a carousel of twelve. Fewer things to find, far fewer reasons to hesitate.</p><p><strong>Nothing pre ticked.</strong> Pre ticking lifts AOV on day one, then costs trust and returns.</p><p><strong>Print over availability.</strong> If a matching piece sells out, the row stays and cannot be ticked. No silent swap to another print, because the print is the reason to buy.</p><p><strong>The dip stays outside the set.</strong> At ₹149 it cannot unlock the offer, so it gets its own spot and says so before the tap.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="changed" class="lt wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">What changed, and how I would measure it</p>
          <h2>From three separate decisions to one.</h2>
        </div>
        <table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col"><span class="vh">Part of the page</span></th><th scope="col">Before</th><th scope="col">After</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Where the kit sits</td><td data-l="Before">Below Add to cart, the trust row and the offers box</td><td data-l="After" class="aft">Right after the print picker, above the button</td></tr>
            <tr><td>How the offer reads</td><td data-l="Before">Two rules in a box: Buy 2 get 5%, Buy 3 get 10%</td><td data-l="After" class="aft">A bar the lunchbox already starts filling</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Buying the full set</td><td data-l="Before">Three items, three Add to cart buttons, no running total</td><td data-l="After" class="aft">One button: Add 3 to cart · ₹5,757</td></tr>
            <tr><td>What the parent sees saved</td><td data-l="Before">Nothing until the cart</td><td data-l="After" class="aft">₹170, then ₹640, live as pieces are ticked</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The strongest match</td><td data-l="Before">The Wander bag in Pixie Dash is missing from the kit</td><td data-l="After" class="aft">The bag is the third piece of the set</td></tr>
            <tr><td>The ₹149 dip</td><td data-l="Before">Silently does not count toward the offer</td><td data-l="After" class="aft">Says so up front, sits where its question is asked</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <h3 class="sub">The test</h3>
        <div class="test">
          <div class="setup"><span class="k">Setup</span><p>50/50 A/B test on PDP traffic for two full weeks, so both weekends and a school week are covered.</p></div>
          <div><span class="k">Primary</span><p>Share of orders with 2 or more items, starting from 30%. Items per order.</p></div>
          <div><span class="k">Secondary</span><p>AOV, units per order, and set attach rate by print.</p></div>
          <div><span class="k">Guardrails</span><p>PDP Add to cart rate and conversion must not drop. Return rate and discount cost per order are watched, since more orders now hit 10%.</p></div>
        </div>
        <details class="fold" open><summary><h3 class="sub">Assumptions to check before build</h3></summary>
        <div class="goals plain">
          <div><p>The Pickle Dip physically fits the ACE 3C snack compartment. If not, the card pairs by use instead of by fit.</p></div>
          <div><p>Which prints have a matching bottle and bag in stock. That list decides when the no match state shows.</p></div>
          <div><p>Buy 2 and Buy 3 apply automatically at checkout on the sale price. Every saving on the page is calculated that way.</p></div>
          <div><p>On Bento Neo pages the builder offers the existing Neo plus HydraPop bundle as the set, so it never competes with a SKU Basil already sells.</p></div>
        </div></details>
      </section>

      <section id="learned" class="lt wide">
        <div class="intro">
          <p class="eyebrow">What I learned</p>
          <h2>Most of the work was deciding what not to add.</h2>
        </div>
        <div class="list">
          <div><h3>The incentive existed. It just was not doing its job.</h3><p>Before designing a new discount, check whether the one already being paid for is visible at the moment of decision. Here the fix was placement and framing, not money.</p></div>
          <div><h3>The easiest upsell was the wrong one to lead with.</h3><p>The ₹149 dip is the cheapest add on, and it looks like the easy win. It cannot unlock the offer, so putting it in the set would have made the bar feel like it lied. It moved to where its real question gets asked, with the exception stated.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Removing things is part of the design.</h3><p>The offers box, the old kit block and the looping timer all came out. One place that fully explains the deal beats three places that each explain half of it.</p></div>
          <div><h3>Edge cases are where the trust is kept.</h3><p>Sold out prints, items already in the cart, name printed boxes that cannot be returned. A naive version double counts or overpromises in each one, so I designed them before calling the main state done.</p></div>
          <div class="nx"><span class="n">Next</span><h3>One for each kid.</h3><p>A second box in another print, for families with siblings. Parked on purpose, and the first thing I would test once the set is live.</p></div>
        </div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>

  <div class="endsec">
  <footer class="closing">
    <h2>Basil</h2>
    <p>Sell the set, not the second product.</p>
